uld  splinter  but we haven't and we're not going to  good messages that we all made to stay  united  but protesters insist climate change  must also be at the top of the g7 agenda  and leaders here will indeed spend time  talking about that tackling climate  change is both a challenge and a massive  opportunity  on climate the eu is the leader and we  are fully committed to the paris  agreement still with those fresh rocket  attacks on keeves sunday ukraine remains  the most urgent topic on the table here  right now and on monday the ukrainian  president himself will join in vladimir  zelinski will dial in by video  conference to talk to justin trudeau and  other g7 leaders about what more the g7  can do to support ukraine in its war  against russia pharah  david aiken in germany thank you david  ahead a retired conservative senator  speaks out critical of her own party in  this global news exclusive the one event  that really changed things for her  you're watching global national  a high-profile veteran of the  conservative party is speaking out over  its future in an exclusive interview on  the west block former conservative  senate leader marjorie lebreton  expressed worries for the party she's  worked with for decades over the  divisive direction it now seems to be  heading as abigail beeman reports  lebreton is pushing back against the  dangerous rhetoric growing popular among  its members including those running for  leadership  i'm very worried for our party marjorie  lebreton retired conservative senate  leader decades of experience with the  party and widely respected in an  exclusive interview on the west block  she says she feels she no longer has a  home in the conservative party i really  fear that the great accommodation that  was reached between stephen harper and  peter mckay  in the in the fall of 2003  is is fracturing beyond repair causing  her the most concern members who embrace  convoy protests  the whole idea of  wrecking a cornerstone of conservatism  in law and order was sort of really  really upsets me and i'm very very  worried last week key organizers were  welcomed by some conservative mps on  parliament hill including leadership  hopeful leslie lewis  there are further rallies planned for  canada day and the summer the main  cornerstones of conservatism is law and  order and law and order is law and order  and illegal blockades are illegal  blockades whether they're at  a border crossing a pipeline a railway a  railway line they're illegal lebreton is  also disappointed in the current  leadership race wanting candidates to  focus more on issues less on personal  attacks it's never great when you have  something like senator lebreton  expressing concerns but at the same time  i'm less worried about permanent  divisions in the party i think whoever  wins the leadership will  try to bring people together to heal  those divisions  lebreton resigned from leadership  candidate pierre paliev's riding board  of directors over his support of the  convoy global news reached out to his  campaign for comment but did not get a  response abigail beeman global news  ottawa  next up the scenes we haven't seen in  two years in canada in-person pride at  one of the biggest parades in the world  [Music]  [Music]  this is the first weekend of summer and  oh it certainly feels like it across  much of british columbia the majority of  the province is under a special weather  statement for heat with temperatures  reaching the low to mid 30's today in  metro vancouver the warmer the normal  weather is expected to stick around  until at least tomorrow  and a very different scene in  neighboring alberta as get this bamps  sunshine village ski resort prepares to  reopen for the canada day long weekend  the resort is reporting 10 centimeters  of fresh snow in the past week adding to  the more than 900 centimeters of snow  the mountain received this season  operators say skiers and snowboarders  will be welcomed back on tuesday with  the hopes of staying open until at least  july 3rd  now there are countless reasons to  celebrate  lgbtq2s plus communities this pride  month of course but it wouldn't be  complete without going to the pride  parade well toronto hit quite a  milestone with its own celebration not  only is it now 50 years since the first  pride parade in toronto this was the  first in-person one since 2019. megan  king has more on the festivities  beads were flying and people were  dancing along the downtown parade route  on sunday as toronto celebrated its 41st  pride parade i'm really excited about  this because we deserve  pride is celebration pride is  passion is everything it's just a fun  event i like coming every year it makes  me feel better and happy and for mothers  such as emily maderick pride events she  says provide a safe space for her  children my children  identify as girl and non-binary so it's  really important for them to get out and  see this representation and just  experience all the love and the  happiness plus we're very into rainbows  this is very important  very important and hopefully we'll send  the message to families to you know  embrace your own  the toronto parade now back after a  two-year pandemic hiatus brought crowds  from across the province and beyond it's  rather amazing because most of us are  from a very small town that's not  mostly  rude people  and  it's just so great to see all the like  pride and rainbows and i'm loving it  love i have a lot of students who um  they struggle with sexuality and just  coming out and it's good to be outside  embrace this and oh my gosh allyship  happy pride  with over 200 contingents and over 200  000 participants even these all the way  from montreal everyone's out here  celebrating pride and love megan king  global news toronto  celebrating pride and love that's what  it's all about happy pride to those of  you who are celebrating it this weekend  and that's global national for this  sunday nigh